  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- $D(R),$ the vector space of all differentiable functions on $R$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R),$ the vector space of all $10\\times 100$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}_{200}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $200$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $D(R)$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of $D(R)$ is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\u2026,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\u2026,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $D(R)$  of size $n,$ which imples that $D(R)$ cannot have finite dimension.     .\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R)$ and ${R}_{200}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R))=10\\cdot 100=1\\,000\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{200}[t])=200+1=201,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
